content.content:
### Offline mode (Gemma 3 1B on device)

The cloud path stays the default. When the browser is offline, or a POST to `/api/turn` or `/api/correct` fails with a network error (fetch rejects, not a 4xx/5xx), the client answers with Gemma 3 1B instruct running in the browser through the MediaPipe LLM Inference API (`@mediapipe/tasks-genai`). Replies from the device carry `source: "local"` and Roleplay shows "offline, noé runs on this device".

What works offline:

| Works | Does not |
|---|---|
| Text roleplay with Noé (typed, no Live voice, no Gradium) | Scene parsing from the camera; Look says "camera needs the network" and offers saved scenes |
| Corrections after the exchange | Gemini Live voice |
| Review and recall, the diary, kept phrases (localStorage) | |

Setup, once per browser:

1. On Hugging Face, open `litert-community/Gemma3-1B-IT`, accept the Gemma licence (the repo is gated) and download `gemma3-1b-it-int4-web.task` (about 700 MB).
2. In alltag, open Settings, section "offline tutor", and choose that file. It is copied into the Origin Private File System and loaded from there; the app never downloads it on its own.
3. Optional: set `VITE_GEMMA_MODEL_URL` to a URL you host the file at; Settings then shows a "download" button instead of the file picker.

Requirements and caveats: needs WebGPU (current Chrome or Edge on desktop or Android) and OPFS; elsewhere Settings says "not supported". The MediaPipe LLM Inference web API is in maintenance mode upstream; fine for a hackathon demo, not a long-term bet. A 1B model is much weaker than the cloud models: prompts are short and French, and its JSON is parsed leniently with safe defaults.

#### Install on your phone

The app ships a service worker, so once installed it opens with no network and the offline tutor is reachable.

- Android Chrome: open the site, then Settings, "on your phone", "add to home screen" (or the browser menu, "Add to home screen").
- iOS: open the site in Safari, tap Share, then "Add to Home Screen".
- The model file is picked once in Settings and stays in the browser storage (OPFS) of that installed app.
- WebGPU: Android Chrome is the tested path. iOS 26 Safari has WebGPU, but MediaPipe on it is untested.

### iOS app (Capacitor)

The same web build runs as a native iOS app (`ios/`, Swift Package Manager, no CocoaPods). The web app on Vercel stays the main deliverable.

Prerequisites: a Mac with Xcode 26 and an Apple ID. A free personal team is enough to run on your own iPhone; the build then expires after 7 days and needs a fresh Run.

```sh
pnpm i
VITE_API_BASE=https://<your-vercel-host> pnpm ios:build   # vite build && cap sync ios
pnpm ios:open                                              # opens ios/App in Xcode
```

In Xcode pick your team under Signing & Capabilities, select your iPhone, press Run.

- `VITE_API_BASE` must be set (in `.env` or inline as above) before `ios:build`. Without it the native app calls its own origin (`https://localhost/api/...`) and every request fails.
- On that Vercel deployment set `ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://localhost`, otherwise CORS blocks the app and the token routes answer 403.
- Camera and microphone prompts appear on first use.
- Limits: WebGPU inside WKWebView on iOS 26 is untested, so the on-device Gemma fallback may not work in the native shell. The PWA on Android Chrome is the tested offline path.

// @vitest-environment node
import { readFileSync, readdirSync } from "node:fs";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { describe, expect, it } from "vitest";

const root = join(__dirname, "..");
const readme = readFileSync(join(root, "README.md"), "utf8");

function routes(): string[] {
  return readdirSync(join(root, "api"), { withFileTypes: true })
    .filter((e) => e.isFile() && e.name.endsWith(".ts") && !e.name.endsWith(".test.ts"))
    .map((e) => e.name.replace(/\.ts$/, ""));
}

function envVars(): string[] {
  return readFileSync(join(root, ".env.example"), "utf8")
    .split("\n")
    .map((l) => l.trim())
    .filter((l) => l && !l.startsWith("#"))
    .map((l) => l.split("=")[0].trim())
    .filter(Boolean);
}

describe("README", () => {
  it("names every api route", () => {
    const names = routes();
    expect(names.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
    for (const name of names) expect(readme).toContain(`/api/${name}`);
  });

  it("names every env var from .env.example", () => {
    const vars = envVars();
    expect(vars).toContain("GEMINI_API_KEY");
    for (const v of vars) expect(readme).toContain(v);
  });

  it("names every GEMINI_MODEL_* var", () => {
    const models = envVars().filter((v) => v.startsWith("GEMINI_MODEL_"));
    expect(models.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
    for (const v of models) expect(readme).toContain(v);
  });

  it("names the partners", () => {
    for (const word of ["Entire", "Gradium", "Gemini"]) expect(readme).toContain(word);
  });
});

describe("manifest", () => {
  it("parses as JSON with name alltag", () => {
    const manifest = JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(root, "public/manifest.webmanifest"), "utf8")) as {
      name: string;
    };
    expect(manifest.name).toBe("alltag");
  });
});
- `src/voice/useVoice.ts` (`VoiceState = idle | listening | thinking | speaking`): Gradium STT + TTS, used by the Review screen. `thinking` is set between a final transcript and the answer; `speak()` sets `speaking`; `stop()` cuts TTS for barge-in.
- `src/voice/useLive.ts` (`LiveState = idle | connecting | listening | speaking | done | error`): Gemini Live for Noé. Live has no separate thinking phase; the server streams audio as soon as it has it. `done` fires when the model calls the `task_done` tool. `error` switches the Roleplay screen to the text fallback that calls `/api/turn`.
- `correcting` is the `finish()` step in `src/screens/Roleplay.tsx`: it stops the live session, posts every learner line to `/api/correct` in parallel, dedupes the corrections, writes `session.lastExchange` and calls `go("corrections")`.

## Service worker

`vite-plugin-pwa` (workbox `generateSW`) builds `dist/sw.js` with `registerType: "autoUpdate"` (the worker skips waiting and claims clients); `src/main.tsx` registers `/sw.js` itself in production builds, since the plugin's `virtual:pwa-register` would pull in `workbox-window`. It precaches the app shell (`**/*.{js,css,html,svg,woff2}`, files up to 5 MB) and answers navigations with `/index.html`, so the installed app loads with no network. `/api/*` is excluded from the navigate fallback and routed `NetworkOnly`: API responses are never cached, and offline the client falls back to the on-device model instead. The manifest stays `public/manifest.webmanifest` (`manifest: false` in the plugin).

## Origins

On the web the client and `api/` share one origin: `src/lib/api.ts` fetches `/api/<name>` and no CORS is involved. The iOS app (Capacitor, `server.iosScheme: "https"`) loads the bundled build from `https://localhost`, so it is built with `VITE_API_BASE` set to the Vercel host and calls `${VITE_API_BASE}/api/<name>` with `credentials: "omit"`. Every route starts with `applyCors` from `api/_lib/cors.ts`: an `Origin` listed in `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` (exact match, never a wildcard) gets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` echoed back, preflights end with 204. The browser guard on `/api/gradium-token` and `/api/live-token` (`originAllowed`) accepts the same origin or an allowlisted one and still rejects any other cross-site caller with 403.

